\chapter{The Screen Before Windows}
\label{ch:terminals}
\index{terminals}\index{curses}\index{termcap}\index{terminfo}\index{Teletype}

\section{A terminal is a separate participant}

Early Unix users often interacted through a terminal whose behavior was
partly independent of the host. A printing terminal turned characters
into marks on paper. A video terminal maintained a display and interpreted
control sequences. Neither was merely a modern graphical text box with
its decoration removed.

The connection carried a stream of bytes. Some represented printable
characters; others changed terminal state or controlled the communication.
The host also maintained terminal settings that affected how input and
output were processed. The result was a protocol among application,
kernel terminal subsystem, communication link, and device.

Slow output made brevity valuable. A line editor could operate without
redrawing a page. Short command names and compact diagnostics reduced
transmission and typing. These choices later became cultural expectations,
but their original economics were concrete. A convention optimized for
paper and low bandwidth need not be the ideal convention for a new user
on a fast display.

\section{The kernel edits some of the input}
\index{line discipline}\index{canonical mode}\index{termios}

In canonical terminal input, the terminal subsystem can collect a line,
interpret editing characters, and deliver input to the application after
a delimiter. Echoing can make typed characters visible before the
application reads them. Control characters can trigger signals or other
special behavior according to the settings.

This explains a common surprise: pressing a key does not always deliver
one byte immediately to the application. Some work happens before the
application's read completes. Conversely, a full-screen editor or game
may configure a more direct input mode so it can interpret individual
keystrokes itself.

``Raw'' is often used informally for such behavior, but terminal settings
contain several independent choices. Input translation, signal generation,
echo, output processing, minimum byte counts, and timing can be configured
separately. Modern portable code uses the specified termios interface;
historical systems had different controls and structures
\parencite[ch. 18]{apue}.

A program that changes terminal state must restore it when appropriate.
If it crashes or exits incorrectly, the next shell can inherit a confusing
environment: no echo, unexpected line endings, or a display left in an
alternate mode. The terminal remembers state beyond one process's lifetime.
That is why cleanup is part of interface correctness.

\section{Cursor addressing creates a new application style}

A video terminal that can move its cursor and erase selected regions
supports an entirely different interaction model from a printing terminal.
An editor can show nearby text and change only the affected lines.
A monitor can update a table of values. A menu can highlight a selection
instead of printing another prompt below the previous one.

The difficulty was diversity. Terminals differed in control sequences,
available operations, dimensions, and the cost of an update. Hardcoding
one device's behavior into every application would have reproduced the
portability problem at the display boundary.

Termcap described capabilities so applications could ask how a terminal
performed a particular operation. Terminfo later provided a different
compiled database and associated interfaces. The environment's terminal
type identifies the expected description. A wrong value can produce
nonsense even when the application and emulator are individually working.

Capabilities are more informative than a list of brand names.
An application usually wants to know how to move the cursor, clear a
region, or select an attribute. It need not care which vendor supplied
the device. This is another instance of replacing a concrete identity
with an operational contract.

\section{Curses keeps a model of the display}

Ken Arnold's curses work at Berkeley helped applications use terminals
without handcrafting every update. A curses library can maintain a virtual
screen, compare it with its model of the actual screen, and emit a suitable
sequence of terminal operations. Applications describe the desired result
at a higher level than individual cursor-motion bytes.

The distinction between a virtual window and the physical screen is useful.
An application can update several logical regions, then request a refresh.
The library can combine changes instead of transmitting each intermediate
state. On a slow connection, avoiding redundant output can matter more
than the cost of computing the difference.

Curses is therefore not a graphical window system in the later X sense.
Its windows are regions in a character-cell model within a terminal
connection. They do not automatically correspond to independently managed
client surfaces, graphics buffers, or desktop windows.
The same word names different abstractions at different layers.

Ncurses carried and extended this tradition in free software environments.
Text interfaces remained useful for installers, remote administration,
editors, and monitoring. Their persistence was not simply resistance to
graphics. A character interface can be economical, script-friendly in
some respects, and effective over remote links, provided the user's needs
fit the model.

\section{A pseudoterminal preserves the old agreement}
\index{pseudoterminals}\index{terminal emulators}\index{SSH}

A graphical terminal emulator displays a character-oriented session inside
a modern desktop. On the host side, a pseudoterminal supplies the terminal
interface expected by programs. One end belongs to the application-facing
terminal subsystem; the other can be driven by an emulator or remote-login
service.

This arrangement preserves behavior that a plain pipe does not provide:
terminal settings, job control, foreground process groups, and appropriate
signal interactions. A program can test whether its output is a terminal
and choose color or formatting accordingly. That choice is a convention,
not proof that the output is human-readable in every setting.

SSH can connect a remote session to a pseudoterminal while encrypting and
authenticating the transport. The bytes sent through the session still
include terminal control sequences. Secure transport does not remove the
need to interpret those sequences carefully or sanitize untrusted material
when the application requires it.

The emulator also has its own policies: font selection, scrollback,
clipboard interaction, colors, key mappings, and supported extensions.
A terminal application and its emulator therefore negotiate a living
protocol, even though the visual result resembles an old text screen.
The apparent timelessness conceals decades of implementation change.

\section{Characters are not display cells}
\index{Unicode}\index{accessibility}

ASCII made some early assumptions unusually convenient: many ordinary
printable characters occupied one byte and one cell. International text
breaks the equivalence. An encoding can use several bytes for a code point;
a displayed grapheme can contain several code points; a glyph can occupy
zero, one, or more terminal cells under the terminal's width rules.

Combining marks, East Asian width, bidirectional scripts, and emoji
sequences complicate cursor placement and selection. A program that cuts
a byte string at an arbitrary offset can split an encoded character.
A program that counts code points can still misjudge visual width.
Terminal libraries and emulators must agree closely enough for the display
to remain coherent, and agreement is not universal for every sequence.

Accessibility presents another challenge. A text interface is not
automatically accessible merely because it uses characters.
Repeated screen repainting can obscure meaning for assistive technology.
Color-only distinctions and position-dependent status can hide information.
A line-oriented mode or a clear textual summary may be more usable than
an elaborate full-screen dashboard.

These issues do not make terminal applications obsolete. They show that
an interface optimized for expert local use needs additional design when
its audience and writing systems expand. The same pattern appears later
in graphical desktops and phones.

\section{The terminal's enduring place}

The terminal preserves a compact boundary between a program and a wide
range of display environments. A shell running remotely can remain useful
without knowing the local GPU, monitor resolution, or desktop toolkit.
That separation is extraordinarily valuable for administration and
software development.

Its limits are equally real. Precise typography, rich accessibility
semantics, fluid images, touch input, and complex spatial interaction do
not fit naturally into a character-cell protocol. Extensions can address
some needs, but each extension adds negotiation and compatibility work.
The terminal is a successful specialized interface, not a universal endpoint
for all human interaction.

Bitmap displays made it possible to redraw the interface boundary itself.
Instead of asking which escape sequence moves a cursor, a system could
manage arbitrary pixels, windows, input devices, and independent graphical
clients. Unix's existing separation of processes and networks would shape
how that next environment was built.
